#---
# purpose: "an extern's declared return carries the caller's argument type through a bound variable, not the extern's own body"
#---
#
# # extern_return_binds_to_caller
#
# `dbg/1` forwards to `fz_dbg_value(t) :: t when t: any`, a prelude extern
# with no fz body of its own. An ordinary function's return picks up its
# argument's type because the BODY carries it there; an extern has no body,
# so the same relation has to come from unifying the declared parameter
# against each activation's own input and instantiating the declared return
# through the resulting substitution.
#
# `ascending/0` and `descending/0` are the same two calls in each order. Each
# call keys its own activation of `dbg/1` -- `inputs=[int]` and
# `inputs=[float]` -- and each activation now defines its own return instead
# of both sharing whichever type happened to be interned first, which is what
# made the un-substituted declaration order-dependent.
#
# Paper answer.
#
#   ascending/0   dbg(11); dbg(10.5)   => float
#   descending/0  dbg(10.5); dbg(11)   => int
#
# A function's return is the type of its last expression, so each of these is
# the return of its own final `dbg` call: `dbg(10.5)` for `ascending/0` and
# `dbg(11)` for `descending/0`. The two differ only because each call site's
# activation of the extern instantiates the declared return against its own
# argument. Without that substitution both read the same type and the answer
# depends on which was interned first.
#
# `--dump types` names the program's own functions, so the two lines above are
# what it shows and what this fixture is measured against; the prelude
# activations behind them are not in that view. Execution prints the boxed
# values dbg always prints, in call order.
def ascending() do
  dbg(11)
  dbg(10.5)
end

def descending() do
  dbg(10.5)
  dbg(11)
end

def main() do
  ascending()
  descending()
end
